digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "C5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given composite morphogenesis examples include simultaneous folding+extension and the study focuses on Drosophila mesoderm concomitantly folding+extending, therefore this system is an appropriate model to study coordination of composite morphogenesis."];
  "C6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given a plastic/robust morphogenesis framing and the goal to uncover spatiotemporal coordination mechanisms, therefore the research should focus on identifying spatiotemporal regulators coordinating distinct cytoskeletal networks during morphogenesis."];
  "C8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the two-tier junctional system is controlled by synergistic AP and DV gene patterning systems and a study of coordination aims to uncover spatiotemporal control, therefore upstream patterning is a relevant context for how distinct tiers are specified and coordinated."];
  "I6" [label="(0,0,0) If epithelial morphogenesis is plastic/robust and tissue remodeling relies on force-generating cytoskeletal remodeling, then explaining morphogenesis requires identifying how cytoskeletal force-generating networks are organized and controlled in space and time."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, epithelial morphogenesis during embryonic development is plastic and robust: tissues remodel shape while maintaining structural integrity."];
  "N11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, the goal is to uncover mechanisms that spatiotemporally coordinate formation of distinct cytoskeletal networks enabling simultaneous cell shape and topology changes."];
  "N15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, formation of the two-tier junctional system is controlled by synergistic AP and DV gene patterning systems."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, how the lateral actomyosin network is spatiotemporally controlled is unknown."];
  "N17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, nuclear migration precedes MyoII-LC formation."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, tissue remodelling relies on epithelial cells generating mechanical forces, changing shape/position, while maintaining cell–cell contacts."];
  "N43" [label="(43,0,0) Currently, the nucleus compartmentalizes; its relocation organizes cytoskeleton and RhoGEF2 distribution to establish the second actomyosin tier for intercalation."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, epithelia can simultaneously fold and extend during processes like gastrulation/neurulation/tubulogenesis."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, the study system is Drosophila gastrulation where prospective mesoderm concomitantly folds and extends to address composite morphogenesis coordination."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, mesoderm epithelial cells establish two E-cadherin-tethered actomyosin junctional tiers with distinct functions."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes that during early Drosophila gastrulation, basal migration of mesoderm cell nuclei spatially redistributes RhoGEF2 and reorganizes cortical actomyosin into a lateral, planar-polarized junctional tier, thereby coordinating apical constriction-driven folding with intercalation-driven extension of the epithelial sheet.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Composite epithelial morphogenesis can be studied by linking temporally ordered organelle repositioning to spatial redistribution of cytoskeletal regulators and actomyosin-based junctional remodeling."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Nuclear migration precedes MyoII-LC formation, and nuclear relocation is reported to organize cytoskeleton and RhoGEF2 distribution to establish the second actomyosin tier for intercalation; therefore nuclear relocation is a plausible contributor to lateral actomyosin tier formation."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The temporal precedence of nuclear migration before MyoII-LC formation is consistent with the explanation that nuclear relocation could help control lateral actomyosin recruitment."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Given the goal of identifying spatiotemporal regulators that coordinate distinct cytoskeletal networks during morphogenesis, the mesodermal system with two E-cadherin-tethered actomyosin junctional tiers of distinct function makes the spatiotemporal control of those tiers, including the lateral tier, a relevant object of study."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because the spatiotemporal control of the lateral actomyosin network is unknown, and because the two-tier mesodermal actomyosin system is relevant to morphogenetic coordination, identifying regulators of the lateral actomyosin network is an unresolved problem for explaining composite mesoderm morphogenesis."];
  "I6" -> "C6" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N11" -> "C6" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N1" -> "I6" [label="deduction-case"];
